* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !set_passwd(username)
*
*    set os user password
*
*    This function sets the STATUS() value to 0 if successful and returns true
*    Otherwise sets status to the os status and returns false
*
*    THE PASSWORD IS PROMPTED FOR HERE, not by the operating system.  On
*    Linux this ran "passwd <user>", which does its own prompting on the
*    terminal; Windows has no equivalent that can be driven that way, so SD
*    asks, confirms, and hands the answer to Set-LocalUser.
*
*    Case inversion is turned off around the reads and restored afterwards.
*    LOGIN sets PT$INVERT, which silently upcases everything typed - a
*    password entered as hunter2 arrives as HUNTER2, verifies by hand and
*    fails at login with nothing visibly wrong.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md
*    section 6; this is the same guard logto.step.up uses.
*
*    The password never reaches a command line.  !ps_script writes the script
*    to a file inside the SDSYS directory, which inherits the restricted ACL,
*    runs it and deletes it.  Decision from the repository owner, 14 Aug 2026.
*
*    The account is enabled once the password is set, since CREATE_USER makes
*    it disabled.
*
* status values
*   0 success
*   1 the password could not be set
*   2 the name is not a valid OS user name
*   3 no password entered, or the two did not match
*   5 access denied: SD is not running elevated
*   6 the Windows password was set, but SD's own credential could not be
*     written - the account would be reachable over ssh and refused by the API
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function set_passwd(username)
$catalog !set_passwd

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

$include int$keys.h

pass_set = @false
set.status 0
*
* Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
* Use shared !valid_os_name before OS.EXECUTE (phase 2).
* os.name.chars = '...'
* if username = '' or convert(os.name.chars, '', username) # '' or len(username) > 32 then
if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
   set.status 2
   return pass_set
end
* --------------------

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - was: OS.EXECUTE "sudo passwd " : username
*   Ask here rather than letting the OS ask.  PT$INVERT off around the reads.
   was.inverted = pterm(PT$INVERT, @false)

   display 'New Windows password for ' : username : ': ' :
   input pw1 HIDDEN
   display

   display 'Repeat password: ' :
   input pw2 HIDDEN
   display

   void pterm(PT$INVERT, was.inverted)

   if pw1 = '' or pw1 # pw2 then
      set.status 3
      return pass_set
   end

*  A password is arbitrary text and must not be interpolated.  PowerShell
*  expands $name and backticks inside DOUBLE quotes, so a password containing
*  $env:USERNAME would be substituted rather than set, and one containing a
*  double quote would end the string and change the script.  Inside SINGLE
*  quotes nothing is special; the only character needing care is the
*  apostrophe itself, which PowerShell escapes by doubling.
   q = char(39)
   safe.pw = change(pw1, q, q:q)

   ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }'
   ps := @fm : 'try {'
   ps := @fm : '  $p = ConvertTo-SecureString -String ' : q : safe.pw : q : ' -AsPlainText -Force'
   ps := @fm : '  Set-LocalUser -Name ' : q : username : q : ' -Password $p -ErrorAction Stop'
   ps := @fm : '  Enable-LocalUser -Name ' : q : username : q : ' -ErrorAction Stop'
   ps := @fm : '} catch { exit 1 }'
   ps := @fm : 'exit 0'

   stat = ps_script(ps)
   safe.pw = '' ; ps = ''

* 28 Aug 26 Windows port - SET SD'S OWN CREDENTIAL FROM THE SAME PROMPT.
*   Owner's ruling, 28 Aug 2026, on PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 42.  Setting the
*   Windows password left $cred empty, so CREATE.ACCOUNT printed 10078 - "SD
*   routes for %1: ssh and the API" - over an account the API could not
*   authenticate.  sshd checks the WINDOWS password; the API does SCRAM
*   against $cred, and only MODIFY.PASSWORD ever wrote one.
*
*   MEASURED 28 Aug 2026 on sddr1a, which was in sdapi, sdssh AND sdusers -
*   route granted, credential absent - and the refusal names nothing, because
*   APISRVR:507 answers 10003 for "no such account" and "not granted" too.
*   The account had to be repaired with MODIFY.PASSWORD before the API door
*   could be measured at all.
*
*   THIS FILE'S CALLER ALREADY BELIEVED IT.  CREATEA:537 says an account whose
*   user has no password has "no $cred, which under the SCRAM login is an
*   account nothing can ever connect to" - true of the intent, false of the
*   code, until now.
*
*   ONE PROMPT SETS BOTH.  They remain separate credentials in separate stores;
*   this gives them the same value, which is what an administrator typing "the
*   password for this account" means.  MODIFY.PASSWORD still changes the SD one
*   on its own, and is still the only way to make them differ.
*
*   ORDER MATTERS: Windows first, then $cred.  A $cred written for an account
*   whose Windows user was refused would be a credential for a login that
*   cannot happen.  pw1 is therefore cleared BELOW this block rather than with
*   safe.pw above it.
   if stat = 0 then
      cred.ok = @false
      call !CRED_SET(username, pw1, cred.ok)
      if not(cred.ok) then stat = 6
   end

   pw1 = '' ; pw2 = ''

if stat = 0 then
  pass_set = @TRUE 
end else
  set.status stat
end

return pass_set
end
